TERRAFORM REMOTE BACKEND (S3 – LATEST WITH NATIVE LOCKING)
====================================================================

--------------------------------------------------
WHAT IS TERRAFORM BACKEND?
--------------------------------------------------

    Backend defines where Terraform stores
    its state file (terraform.tfstate).

    Default:
        Local file (not safe for teams)

    Remote Backend:
        Stores state in remote location like:
            • S3
            • Terraform Cloud
            • Azure blob Storage
			. Gcs (goolge cloud storage) 


--------------------------------------------------
WHAT IS REMOTE BACKEND?
--------------------------------------------------

    Remote backend stores Terraform state
    in a shared location.

    Benefits:

        • Team collaboration
        • Centralized state
        • Backup & versioning
        • Secure access


--------------------------------------------------
WHY REMOTE BACKEND IS REQUIRED?
--------------------------------------------------

    Problems with local state:

        • No sharing
        • Risk of deletion
        • No locking
        • Conflicts in team

    Remote backend solves:

        • Shared access
        • Safe updates
        • Locking mechanism


--------------------------------------------------
STATE LOCKING (IMPORTANT)
--------------------------------------------------

    State locking prevents multiple users
    from modifying infrastructure at same time.

    Example:

        User1 → terraform apply
        User2 → blocked until User1 finishes


--------------------------------------------------
OLD APPROACH (DEPRECATED)
--------------------------------------------------

    S3 + DynamoDB

    S3:
        Stores state file

    DynamoDB:
        Handles locking

Example (OLD):

    terraform {
      backend "s3" {
        bucket         = "my-tf-state"
        key            = "dev/terraform.tfstate"
        region         = "ap-south-1"
        dynamodb_table = "terraform-locks"
      }
    }

    NOTE:
        DynamoDB locking is now DEPRECATED.


--------------------------------------------------
NEW APPROACH (TERRAFORM 1.10+)
--------------------------------------------------

    S3 supports native locking.

    No need for DynamoDB.

    Terraform uses:
        • Lock file (.tflock)
        • S3 conditional writes


--------------------------------------------------
NEW BACKEND CONFIGURATION (RECOMMENDED)
--------------------------------------------------

    terraform {
      backend "s3" {
        bucket       = "my-tf-state"
        key          = "dev/terraform.tfstate"
        region       = "ap-south-1"
        use_lockfile = true
      }
    }


--------------------------------------------------
WHAT IS use_lockfile?
--------------------------------------------------

    Enables native S3 locking.

    When terraform apply runs:

        • Lock file created in S3
        • Other users blocked
        • Lock released after completion


--------------------------------------------------
S3 SETUP STEPS
--------------------------------------------------

    Step 1: Create S3 bucket

        Name: my-tf-state

    Step 2: Enable versioning (IMPORTANT)

        Protects state file history

    Step 3: Configure backend in Terraform


--------------------------------------------------
WORKFLOW
--------------------------------------------------

    terraform init

        • Configures backend
        • Connects to S3
        • Prepares locking

    terraform plan

        • Reads remote state

    terraform apply

        • Acquires lock
        • Updates infrastructure
        • Releases lock


--------------------------------------------------
REAL DEVOPS USE CASE
--------------------------------------------------

    Team of 5 engineers working on same infra.

    Without remote backend:

        ❌ Conflicts
        ❌ Overwrites

    With S3 backend:

        ✅ Shared state
        ✅ Safe updates
        ✅ Locking prevents conflicts


--------------------------------------------------
IMPORTANT NOTES
--------------------------------------------------

    • DynamoDB locking is deprecated
    • Still works but not recommended
    • S3 native locking is future standard
    • Always enable versioning in S3


--------------------------------------------------
INTERVIEW SUMMARY
--------------------------------------------------

    Backend:
        Where Terraform stores state

    Remote Backend:
        Shared storage (S3)

    Old:
        S3 + DynamoDB

    New:
        S3 + use_lockfile

    Locking:
        Prevents concurrent changes


--------------------------------------------------
FINAL SUMMARY
--------------------------------------------------

    S3 → State storage
    S3 → Locking (new)

    Best Practice:
        Use S3 with use_lockfile enabled

