for each concpet 

====================================================================
1. WHAT IS for_each IN TERRAFORM?
====================================================================

`for_each` is a Terraform meta-argument.

It is used to create multiple instances of a resource or module
using a MAP or SET of values.

Instead of writing the same resource block multiple times,
we can write one resource block and use for_each.

WHY DO WE USE for_each?

1. To create multiple AWS resources.
2. To avoid duplicate Terraform code.
3. To manage resources individually.
4. To use different names and configurations for each resource.
5. To make infrastructure code reusable and scalable.

EXAMPLE:

Suppose we need to create 3 S3 buckets:

- dev bucket
- test bucket
- prod bucket

Without for_each:
We need to write 3 separate resource blocks.

With for_each:
We write only one resource block.

====================================================================
2. for_each SYNTAX
====================================================================

resource "RESOURCE_TYPE" "RESOURCE_NAME" {
  for_each = MAP_OR_SET

  ARGUMENT = each.value
}

IMPORTANT:

for_each  = tells Terraform to create multiple instances.
each.key  = current map key or set element.
each.value = current map value or set element.

Terraform creates one resource instance for every element.

====================================================================
3. UNDERSTANDING each.key AND each.value
====================================================================

Example:

variable "environment" {
  default = {
    dev  = "development"
    test = "testing"
    prod = "production"
  }
}

Here:

each.key   = dev, test, prod
each.value = development, testing, production

Terraform processes each entry separately.

====================================================================
4. REAL-TIME EXAMPLE 1: CREATE MULTIPLE S3 BUCKETS
====================================================================

REQUIREMENT:

Create three S3 buckets for:

1. Development
2. Testing
3. Production

main.tf

resource "aws_s3_bucket" "buckets" {

  for_each = toset([
    "dev",
    "test",
    "prod"
  ])

  bucket = "my-company-${each.key}-bucket"

}

WHAT HAPPENS?

Terraform creates:

aws_s3_bucket.buckets["dev"]
aws_s3_bucket.buckets["test"]
aws_s3_bucket.buckets["prod"]

Actual bucket names:

my-company-dev-bucket
my-company-test-bucket
my-company-prod-bucket

NOTE:

When using a SET of strings:

each.key   = current string
each.value = current string

====================================================================
5. REAL-TIME EXAMPLE 2: CREATE MULTIPLE EC2 INSTANCES
====================================================================

REQUIREMENT:

Create EC2 instances for:

- dev
- test
- prod

Each environment needs a different instance type.

main.tf

variable "instances" {
  default = {
    dev  = "t3.micro"
    test = "t3.small"
    prod = "t3.medium"
  }
}

resource "aws_instance" "servers" {

  for_each = var.instances

  ami           = "ami-xxxxxxxx"
  instance_type = each.value

  tags = {
    Name        = "${each.key}-server"
    Environment = each.key
  }

}

EXPLANATION:

each.key:
dev, test, prod

each.value:
t3.micro, t3.small, t3.medium

Terraform creates:

dev-server  -> t3.micro
test-server -> t3.small
prod-server -> t3.medium

NOTE:

Replace the AMI ID with an AMI valid for your AWS region.

====================================================================
6. REAL-TIME EXAMPLE 3: CREATE MULTIPLE SECURITY GROUPS
====================================================================

REQUIREMENT:

Create separate Security Groups for:

- Web
- Application
- Database

variable "security_groups" {
  default = {
    web  = "Web server security group"
    app  = "Application security group"
    db   = "Database security group"
  }
}

resource "aws_security_group" "sg" {

  for_each = var.security_groups

  name        = "${each.key}-sg"
  description = each.value
  vpc_id      = "vpc-xxxxxxxx"

}

Terraform creates:

web-sg
app-sg
db-sg

WHY IS THIS USEFUL?

In real projects, different application tiers need different
Security Groups.

For example:

Web SG  -> HTTP/HTTPS
App SG  -> Application port
DB SG   -> MySQL/PostgreSQL port

The actual ingress and egress rules must also be configured.

====================================================================
7. REAL-TIME EXAMPLE 4: CREATE MULTIPLE IAM USERS
====================================================================

REQUIREMENT:

Create IAM users for different teams.

variable "iam_users" {
  default = toset([
    "developer",
    "tester",
    "admin"
  ])
}

resource "aws_iam_user" "users" {

  for_each = var.iam_users

  name = each.key

}

Terraform creates:

developer
tester
admin

REAL-TIME USE CASE:

In an organization, different teams may need separate IAM users.

IMPORTANT:

Creating an IAM user does not automatically grant permissions.
Policies or group memberships must be configured separately.

====================================================================
8. for_each WITH MAP OF OBJECTS
====================================================================

This is very useful in real-time projects.

We can provide multiple values for every resource.

Example:

variable "ec2_instances" {
  default = {
    web = {
      instance_type = "t3.micro"
      environment   = "dev"
    }

    app = {
      instance_type = "t3.small"
      environment   = "test"
    }

    db = {
      instance_type = "t3.medium"
      environment   = "prod"
    }
  }
}

resource "aws_instance" "servers" {

  for_each = var.ec2_instances

  ami           = "ami-xxxxxxxx"
  instance_type = each.value.instance_type

  tags = {
    Name        = each.key
    Environment = each.value.environment
  }

}

UNDERSTANDING:

each.key
  web
  app
  db

each.value.instance_type
  t3.micro
  t3.small
  t3.medium

each.value.environment
  dev
  test
  prod

WHY USE MAP OF OBJECTS?

Because one resource may need multiple different settings.

For example:

- Instance type
- Environment
- Disk size
- Application name
- Availability Zone

We can keep all related settings together.

====================================================================
9. for_each WITH MODULES
====================================================================

We can also use for_each with Terraform modules.

Example:

module "s3_buckets" {

  source = "./modules/s3"

  for_each = toset([
    "dev",
    "test",
    "prod"
  ])

  bucket_name = "my-company-${each.key}-bucket"

}

WHAT HAPPENS?

Terraform creates multiple instances of the same module.

module.s3_buckets["dev"]
module.s3_buckets["test"]
module.s3_buckets["prod"]

REAL-TIME USE CASE:

When the same infrastructure needs to be deployed for
multiple environments or applications.

====================================================================
10. IMPORTANT RULES OF for_each
====================================================================

1. for_each supports MAP and SET OF STRINGS.

2. Lists cannot be passed directly to for_each.
   Convert a list of strings using toset() when appropriate.

3. Map keys or set values must be known before Terraform
   performs remote resource operations.

4. Sensitive values cannot be used directly as for_each
   instance keys because keys appear in Terraform plans
   and resource addresses.

5. Each instance is tracked separately in Terraform state.

6. each.key and each.value are available inside the
   resource or module block using for_each.

7. Changing a map key or set element changes the resource
   instance address. Terraform may plan to destroy and
   recreate that instance unless you use a suitable
   state move or migration.

====================================================================
11. for_each VS count
====================================================================

count:

- Uses a number.
- Creates resources with numeric indexes.
- Access instances using count.index.
- Useful when creating similar numbered resources.

Example:

resource "aws_instance" "server" {
  count         = 3
  ami           = "ami-xxxxxxxx"
  instance_type = "t3.micro"
}

Resource addresses:

aws_instance.server[0]
aws_instance.server[1]
aws_instance.server[2]


for_each:

- Uses a MAP or SET OF STRINGS.
- Creates resources with named keys.
- Access instances using each.key and each.value.
- Useful when resources have meaningful names or different settings.

Example:

resource "aws_instance" "server" {
  for_each = toset(["dev", "test", "prod"])

  ami           = "ami-xxxxxxxx"
  instance_type = "t3.micro"
}

Resource addresses:

aws_instance.server["dev"]
aws_instance.server["test"]
aws_instance.server["prod"]


EASY DIFFERENCE:

count    = Create resources using numbers.
for_each = Create resources using meaningful keys or values.

====================================================================
12. HOW TO ACCESS for_each RESOURCE OUTPUTS
====================================================================

Example:

resource "aws_s3_bucket" "buckets" {

  for_each = toset(["dev", "test", "prod"])

  bucket = "my-company-${each.key}-bucket"

}

To access one bucket:

aws_s3_bucket.buckets["dev"].id

To get all bucket IDs:

values(aws_s3_bucket.buckets)[*].id

To get all bucket names:

[for bucket in aws_s3_bucket.buckets : bucket.bucket]

IMPORTANT:

A resource using for_each is represented as a map of
resource instances.

====================================================================
13. TERRAFORM COMMANDS
====================================================================

terraform init

Initializes the working directory and downloads providers.

terraform fmt

Formats Terraform files.

terraform validate

Checks Terraform configuration syntax and consistency.

terraform plan

Shows the resources Terraform plans to create, update,
or destroy.

terraform apply

Applies the planned infrastructure changes.

terraform state list

Lists resources tracked in Terraform state.

terraform destroy

Destroys resources managed by the configuration.

====================================================================
14. COMMON REAL-TIME DEVOPS USE CASES
====================================================================

USE CASE 1: MULTIPLE ENVIRONMENT BUCKETS

Create separate S3 buckets for:

- dev
- test
- staging
- prod

USE CASE 2: MULTIPLE EC2 SERVERS

Create:

- web servers
- application servers
- batch processing servers

Each can have a different instance type or configuration.

USE CASE 3: MULTIPLE SECURITY GROUPS

Create separate Security Groups for:

- ALB
- Web
- Application
- Database

